</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Work Completed</h2>
<p>
    The first phase examined Microsoft's PE ratio over an eight-year window, compared PE and revenue trends against
    five technology-sector peers, reviewed Q1 and Q2 2020 COVID-period stock behavior, and visualized revenue
    distribution across the selected companies by year. That phase emphasized data cleaning, chart construction,
    and written analysis that supported the conclusions.
</p>
<p>
    The machine-learning phase added monthly inflation and earnings analysis, train/test split comparison, sector
    price analysis against inflation and consumer confidence, unemployment-rate analysis as an additional macroeconomic
    hypothesis, and model comparison for Microsoft closing prices.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Data Sources And Fallback Handling</h2>
<p>
    The notebook uses live public data rather than a static class dataset. It retrieves market data with yfinance,
    macroeconomic data from FRED through pandas-datareader, annual financial data through SEC Company Facts, and
    historical price fallback data from Stooq.
</p>
<p>
    That source mix creates practical data-engineering concerns: public endpoints can rate-limit, return incomplete
    data, revise historical values, or fail temporarily. The project documents those limitations and includes fallback
    paths so the analysis is more resilient than a one-source notebook.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">What It Demonstrates</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Retrieved public financial and market data with yfinance, SEC Company Facts, Stooq, FRED, and pandas-datareader.</li>
    <li>Prepared and aligned time-series, financial-statement, stock-price, and macroeconomic data with pandas and NumPy.</li>
    <li>Analyzed PE ratios, revenue trends, COVID-period stock behavior, peer technology stocks, and sector-level metrics.</li>
    <li>Built visualizations with Matplotlib to support valuation, distribution, trend, and model-output analysis.</li>
    <li>Compared linear regression, multiple linear regression, polynomial regression, and random forest regression models.</li>
    <li>Documented assumptions, limitations, source volatility, and analytical findings in the notebook narrative.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Analysis Scope</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Microsoft PE ratio and earned-revenue comparison.</li>
    <li>Peer-company PE ratio comparison across five related technology stocks.</li>
    <li>Five-number summary visualization of recent stock prices.</li>
    <li>COVID-period stock behavior review for Q1 and Q2 2020.</li>
    <li>Revenue-distribution visualization across selected stocks by year.</li>
    <li>Microsoft earnings compared against monthly inflation.</li>
    <li>Sector valuation analysis against inflation and consumer confidence.</li>
    <li>Unemployment-rate analysis as an additional macroeconomic variable.</li>
    <li>Linear regression, polynomial regression, and random forest comparison for Microsoft closing prices.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Finance And Risk Analytics Relevance</h2>
<p>
    The value of this project is in the workflow it demonstrates: pulling imperfect public data, handling source
    fallbacks, deriving metrics, comparing peer entities, testing relationships between variables, and explaining
    what the results do and do not support.
</p>
<p>
    Those skills map well to investigative analytics and risk-oriented data work because the notebook has to reconcile
    inconsistent sources, make transformations explicit, and communicate limitations instead of treating model output
    as automatically authoritative.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Machine-Learning Scope</h2>
<p>
    The machine-learning portion extends the finance analysis by testing relationships between Microsoft earnings,
    inflation, consumer confidence, unemployment, and technology-sector price behavior. It also compares Microsoft
    closing-price predictions from linear regression, polynomial regression, and random forest regression.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Repository Workflow</h2>
<p>
    The repository includes a requirements file and a GitHub Actions workflow that installs dependencies, clears stale
    notebook output, reruns the notebook, retries temporary public-data failures, and commits refreshed notebook output.
    That workflow is useful because several upstream data sources can change, rate-limit requests, or return temporary
    endpoint issues.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Important Limitations</h2>
<p>
    The project is educational and portfolio-oriented. It is not financial advice, investment advice, or a production
    financial risk model. The calculations are simplified and depend on live public data availability.
</p>
<p>
    A production version would need stronger source controls, audited financial inputs, consistent share-count
    methodology, repeatable data snapshots, and more rigorous validation. The model outputs should be read as
    demonstrations of analytical technique rather than reliable stock-price forecasts.
</p>

